// Layout primitive: the annotated state holds a variable number of element
// slots, stored out of line. It targets State because its slots change; @many
// on a let is rejected. Its declared type supplies the element type; C uses
// only that type's layout, never its name. Macros such as @collection apply
// @many; user declarations do not.
@builtin
macro many(): State

// @many has no operations of its own. The construct that owns the member
// declares the slot primitives as builtin functions, and C implements them on
// that construct's single @many member:
//
//   @builtin function resize(let to: Int)             make exactly n slots available
//   @builtin function read(let at: Int): Element      the element in slot i
//   @builtin function write(let at: Int, let value: Element)
//   @builtin function drop(let at: Int)               remove slot i; the slots after it move down one,
//                                                     and the last slot is left empty
//
// @many does not track count, capacity, or growth policy; the owning macro
// declares those as ordinary members. The member is not a standalone value.
//
// When the element type is a macro type, as in Array<@fruit>, C partitions
// the slots by type: one block per type applying the macro, holding its
// values in their order among the elements, and one order list of (type,
// index) per slot. The primitives behave the same; reading a slot is O(1),
// and writing another type into one, or dropping one, is O(n). Each type's
// block grows by doubling, a policy C holds.
//
// Open: behavior for out-of-range slots; layout computation for Element;
// the allocator behind resize.
